WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.980
- [Narrator] In some
ways, the Federal Reserve

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.240
is like a company.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.850
It has a balance sheet with assets

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.890
that bring in money on one side

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:11.130
and liabilities that
cost money on the other.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.290
But what the Fed keeps
on its balance sheet

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.930
has ramifications for the economy.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.240
- The balance sheet is
the tool that they use

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.270
to control and manage interest rates.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.190
- [Narrator] And for
the last 15 or so years,

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.500
the Federal Reserve
has actually made money

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.500
off of this balance sheet... until today.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.380
Thanks to, well, the Fed's own policies.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:32.213
- They're losing money

00:00:32.213 --> 00:00:34.020
because they're raising
interest rates a lot.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.640
- [Narrator] Here's
how the Fed makes money

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.353
and why they're not now.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.610
This is what their
balance sheet looks like.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:42.720
On the asset side,

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.840
they have US treasury
securities like bonds.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.870
They're considered really
stable, low-risk investments.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.720
And the Fed buys them,
sometimes, a lot of them,

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.490
to help stabilize the economy.

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:54.930
- The point of it is to bring down

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.040
longer-term interest rates,

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.710
so if more people are
purchasing these securities,

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.120
in this case the Fed,

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.980
that's gonna drive down the interest rate,

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.110
there's more demand for the bond.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.130
- [Narrator] And the Fed purchases

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:06.810
mortgage-backed securities too,

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.550
which are what they sound like.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:09.660
When they invest in them,

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.360
they're basically investing
in people's mortgages.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.940
- If you have a 2.5% mortgage rate,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.620
that was in part because the Fed

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.860
was buying mortgage-backed
securities during the pandemic.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.360
- [Narrator] So that's the asset side,

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.030
which totals nearly $9 trillion.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:27.510
On the liability side, the
Federal Reserve has literally

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.210
all of the currency in circulation.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:33.630
It's why all money has Federal
Reserve Note written on it.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:37.200
It's a liability of the
Fed, the US Central Bank.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.810
The Fed is also a bank for other banks,

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.030
and their main liability
is reserves of money

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.370
that other banks keep at the Fed.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.340
It's another way they can
influence the economy.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:48.780
- There's some concern that if you had

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.790
all of this money out there,

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.130
you could have even more inflation,

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.860
you could have economic
effects that you don't intend.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:00.420
So one of the ways to prevent
that money from spurring

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.560
even more demand is to have
banks hold it at the Fed,

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.720
and the Fed controls interest rates

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.560
by setting the rate of
interest on those reserves.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:11.460
- [Narrator] When the Fed raises

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.200
interest rates on these reserves,

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.160
it affects other markets,
from bank loans to mortgages.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.380
But really, it means the Federal Reserve

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:23.040
has to pay more in
interest to those banks.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.780
For years, that amount was
really low, usually below 1%.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.780
Yet, the Fed's assets, on
average, paid 2.3% in interest.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.220
So the Fed's assets were bringing in

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.730
more interest than its
liabilities were costing.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.040
The Fed was actually turning a profit,

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.550
and it would send that
profit to the US Treasury.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.280
In the last 11 years,
this profit would range

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.850
from about 4 to $12 billion a month.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.220
- The money that the Fed
sends to the Treasury

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:54.960
means the Treasury doesn't have to raise

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.850
as much money in the private market.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.920
It means the borrowing needs

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.840
of the US government are smaller,

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.840
it means deficits are lower
than they would otherwise be.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.430
- [Narrator] But now
that the Federal Reserve

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:09.390
is raising interest rates,
that profit is now a loss.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.640
- They are raising interest rates so much

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.820
that they're gonna pay
more out than they take in.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.430
- [Narrator] This idea of
the Fed even having a profit

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.920
or loss is a relatively new one.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.130
Before 2008, the Fed's balance
sheet looked like this,

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:26.040
about $1 trillion in
assets and liabilities,

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.540
mainly treasury securities

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.240
to cover the currency in circulation.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.880
But then, the 2008 financial crisis hit.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.130
They dropped interest rates to zero,

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.780
but wanted to do even more,

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.300
so they bought treasury and
mortgage-backed securities

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.000
to push interest rates down even further.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.030
That began a decade of growth
of the Fed's balance sheet

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.620
as interest rates stayed low

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.750
and the Central Bank utilized these assets

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.750
and liabilities to influence the economy.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.450
Although they tried to shrink
the balance sheet at times...

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:56.010
- The plan was never to permanently

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:57.270
expand the balance sheet.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.380
Then COVID comes,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.810
and there was a financial
panic in March, 2020.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:04.110
And they bought just an
enormous, staggering amount

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.650
of treasury securities and
mortgage-backed securities.

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:09.120
- [Narrator] So with a large balance sheet

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.990
comes large profits, but
now also large losses.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:15.510
- So, when the Fed loses
money, what happens?

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:19.950
They create basically an IOU
where they will pay back later

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.720
the money that they're losing now,

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.730
they call this a deferred asset.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.670
- [Narrator] Basically, they'll
pay themselves back later.

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.290
The Treasury will have less money,

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:29.670
which means they'll have to take

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.130
on more debt to add to the deficit.

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:33.720
But for the Federal Reserve,

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.930
experts say it shouldn't change
their day-to-day operations,

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:38.940
and they're not too worried about it.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.490
- The Fed's pretty clear,
their goals are to achieve

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:44.430
low and stable inflation
and strong employment.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:46.440
It doesn't say anything
anywhere that they're supposed

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.660
to make a lot of money
for the US government.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:50.760
And so, that's how they're
treating this right now.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.350
- [Narrator] Now that
the Fed has accumulated

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:55.470
such a large balance sheet
to manage the economy,

00:04:55.470 --> 00:05:00.284
it must manage not just its
gains, but also it's losses.

00:05:00.284 --> 00:05:02.867
(gentle music)

